<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeseník</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Freiwaldau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeseník (German: Freiwaldau) is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> Region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, in the Jeseníky mountains near the Polish border. It belonged to the episcopal Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a> and remained with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took most of the duchy in 1742. The spa at nearby Gräfenberg, founded by Vincenz Priessnitz in the 1820s, made it a centre of hydrotherapy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesenik.jpg" alt="Jeseník" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomáš Neuwirth · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masarykovo_n%C3%A1m%C4%9Bst%C3%AD_v_Jesen%C3%ADku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jeseník lies about 70 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, in the historical land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a>, at the confluence of the Bělá River and the Staříč Stream. The southern part of its territory lies in the Hrubý Jeseník mountain range and the northern part extends to the Golden Mountains. The original name, Freiwaldau or Frývaldov, comes from the German frei vom Walde, meaning free from the woods. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the town took its present name from the surrounding mountains, called Hrubý Jeseník or Jeseníky.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written mention dates from 1267. The town was probably founded during the colonisation of the area by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, who acquired it in 1199, and it gained town status between 1284 and 1295, helped by its position on a trade route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> to Silesia and by iron ore deposits. In 1326 thirteen hammer mills were already working iron. From 1378 to 1463 the Mušín family owned it, and in 1506 the Swabian Fugger family bought it and resumed mining. They sold it back to the Wrocław bishops in 1547, and linen weaving became its most important source of income.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> damaged the town several times, and between 1622 and 1684 it stood at the centre of the Northern Moravia witch trials. A fire in 1696 caused severe damage. In 1742, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-silesian-war">First Silesian War</a>, it became part of Austrian Silesia and lost its Silesian markets, so that the linen trade fell into crisis. Industrialisation brought new factories in the 19th century, among them a textile works founded in 1822, and a railway opened in 1888. In 1938 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> ceded the town to Nazi Germany, and in 1945 it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and its German population was expelled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jeseník has about 10,000 inhabitants and is known for the Priessnitz Medical Spa, founded by Vincenz Priessnitz in 1822 as the world's first hydrotherapy institute; his birth house is now a museum, and his tomb dates from 1853. The Gräfenberg hamlet, founded in 1808, was incorporated into the town in 1868. The oldest monument is the Jeseník Water Fortress, a Gothic castle of the 13th century first documented in 1374, which houses the regional museum. The town hall dates from 1710.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeseník” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1742 most of the duchy passed to Prussia, which secularised it in 1810, while the southern part around <b>Jeseník</b> remained Austrian under the bishops until 1850.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-nysa">Duchy of Nysa</a></li></ul></section>
